{
 "text": "Everyone building with AI keeps saying commit, push and branch. This is what those words actually mean. GitHub runs on Git, the version control tool almost every developer uses. Here's how it works. Every time you save your progress, that's called a commit. It's a snapshot of every file in your project, linked to the one before it. Line them up, and that's your app's timeline. So when a prompt breaks something, you tell Claude to go back to the last version that worked. But that timeline only lives on your laptop. If that dies, it's gone. So you push it to GitHub, and the whole history lives online. Grabbing a tool from there does the same thing in reverse. Cloning copies its entire timeline onto your computer. Want to try a new feature without risking the version that works? Make a branch, a second timeline that splits off. If it turns out well, you merge it back in. If not, you delete it. And when a version is solid, tag it, like v1, so you can come back to it anytime. If you're new to building with AI, just tell Claude to commit and push. It runs the commands for you. Comment COMMIT and I'll send you the exact prompts to set up GitHub with Claude Code.",
